---
title: Funding Radio Across Scales
authors: [Kaela Godfrey, Rhoda Ayele]
broadcast: 5
order: 5
start: "16:56"
---

NPR, WFMU, and the Cornell Media Guild represent different approaches to sustaining radio. All three rely on a combination of institutional, community, and earned support; but what distinguishes them is where that support comes from and how directly a station’s financial survival is tied to the community it serves. NPR operates within a large public media network, with funding distributed across federal institutions, member stations, corporate sponsors, foundations, and listeners. WFMU, by contrast, relies much more directly on its listening community, making fundraising, events, merchandise, and audience participation essential parts of the station’s success. The Cornell Media Guild falls somewhere between these models, combining commercial broadcasting with student media, alumni support, donations, and events. These different funding structures shape how closely the station is connected to its audience and what kinds of physical, social, and cultural infrastructure it is able to sustain. In this way, funding becomes not just a means of supporting radio, but part of how each institution builds and maintains its community.

NPR sits within a public media system supported in part by the Corporation for Public Broadcasting (CPB), which distributes federal funding throughout the public broadcasting network. Much of this money goes to local stations, while NPR generates revenue primarily through programming fees paid by its member stations, corporate sponsorships, contributions, and grants. NPR is therefore not simply dependent on federal funding itself, with only about 1% of its funding coming directly from CPB. Its vulnerability is more indirect. When federal support is removed from local stations, those stations have fewer resources to pay for programming and services, creating consequences that can move through the larger network.

WFMU has developed a different model based on a more direct relationship between the station and its listeners. Rather than relying primarily on corporate underwriting or institutional funding, the station asks its audience to actively sustain its programming through donations and fundraising campaigns. Its annual Record Fair extends this relationship into physical space, bringing together record dealers, collectors, musicians, and listeners while generating revenue for the station. Donated records can also become a source of funding, turning objects donated by the station’s musical community into financial resources. Fundraising becomes an event through which the community gathers and actively participates in sustaining the institution.

The Cornell Media Guild provides another variation on this relationship between media, money, and community. The Guild is a student run media ecosystem made up of three platforms: WVBR, Cornell Radio, and Electric Buffalo Records. WVBR is a commercial FM station broadcasting across Ithaca and the surrounding region. Cornell Radio is a free-form online station where students can create their own programs. Electric Buffalo is a student-run record label supporting local and student musicians through recording, releases, and performances. Rather than operating as three completely separate organizations, they share equipment, staff, alumni, and a physical headquarters at their Ithaca location.

The Guild’s independence is closely tied to how it funds this shared infrastructure. It does not receive funding or management from Cornell University. Instead, it combines earned income with alumni and public support. Student produced concerts, open mics, and other events turn the Guild’s Collegetown house into a revenue generating venue, with ticket sales helping to support the building and equipment used across the organization. Donations and alumni support provide another layer of stability. Its network of more than 2,000 alumni has helped support major pieces of infrastructure, including the building, transmitter, and long-term endowment.

Across all three models, physical space becomes an important part of how media institutions sustain themselves and build community. WFMU’s Record Fair transforms fundraising into a gathering. The Cornell Media Guild’s house functions simultaneously as a headquarters, venue, studio, and social space. These spaces demonstrate that the infrastructure of radio includes the places where people meet, perform, exchange objects, donate money, produce music, and participate in the culture surrounding the station. The sustainability of these organizations therefore depends on more than keeping a station on the air. They rely on creating communities that have a reason to keep the institution alive.

